WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.700
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Kathryn Judge, who's the Harvey J. Goldschmidt,

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:10.160
professor of law at Columbia Law School and
also author of the book Direct The Rise of

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.089
the Middlemen Economy and the Power of Going
to the Source. It's really great having you

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:18.660
on. I appreciate your time.
Speaker 3: Yeah, thank you for having me.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.939
Speaker 1: So I think intuitively many in
our audience will understand the concept of

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:32.450
middlemen in the global economy. And my instinct
is that at some point it was quite logical

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:40.560
to start seeing middlemen as we started to
see many industries scale international trade.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.390
Know we could think of reasons why why it
made sense. What are the origins of so many

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.690
middlemen in so many of the industries with
which we regularly engage?

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.310
Speaker 3: I mean, you really hit on a lot
of the drivers for the the middlemen or the

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:58.850
connectors through whom good flow through
money flows from people who have it to those

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:03.880
who need it. And both as the economy got more
global, but also as it got more specialized.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.670
We saw a proliferation of middlemen helping
to facilitate those transfers. So you might

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:15.220
have, you know, investors with excess capital
in one country and limited opportunities for

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:19.710
return. And so you ended up initially as one
and then subsequently oftentimes layers of

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:25.140
middlemen that would help facilitate flows
to where there might be productive undertakings,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.761
even if that meant kind of going moving the
money abroad. Similarly, in production, we

00:01:29.761 --> 00:01:34.280
know games from trade is a is a classic idea.
And of course, we've had a shift from just

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.409
having goods imported from one country to
another to really having globalized supply

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:45.270
chains where goods are made in a multi continent
undertaking with each step kind of undertaking

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:50.670
what it can do most efficiently. But of course,
as you have that disaggregation of production,

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.159
you need middlemen to help connect each step
along the way.

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:57.460
Speaker 1: So before we talk about some of
the downsides, I mean, it seems accurate to

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:04.510
say that in many cases, consumers do see lower
prices on many products as a result of middlemen.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:07.180
Is that fair?
Speaker 3: That's certainly fair. And one

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.540
of the points of the book is actually middlemen
usually arise because they're really providing

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:18.180
significant value. And it explores how overcoming
the informational and logistical challenges

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:23.150
that can otherwise impede transactions that
make everybody better off is a real value

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:28.700
enhancing service to provide. So middlemen
themselves are at this point really the backbone

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:33.400
of the economy that we're not going to go
anywhere soon with. The book explores is in

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.390
a static environment you can look at take
a snapshot and it looks like they're providing

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:42.690
a lot of value and oftentimes they are. But
the very nature of what makes them so good

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:49.520
at their jobs also gives them the ability
to exercised outsized influence on the evolution

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.980
of the market and the evolution of the laws
that are governing the spaces where they're

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:58.910
active that sometimes allows them to extract
more value and and lead to potentially other

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:03.500
challenges in future periods.
Speaker 1: So and when you say that because

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:10.290
if I think of like Walmart and Amazon, Walmart
and Amazon are middlemen in a sense, in that

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.750
they don't manufacture most of the products
they sell. So in a sense, they've created

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.470
this marketplace very convenient for all sorts
of different products, but they also will

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:24.960
contract with other middlemen in many cases
as well. So so they both are middlemen and

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.760
also are not depending on which relationship
we're thinking of.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:33.900
Speaker 3: Yeah. And one of the interesting
point is when you look at the term middleman

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:39.060
itself is very easy to define in the abstract.
But when you're trying to imply that a particular

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.530
company is part of what you realize is a lot
of middlemen, do you provide additional value

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:48.880
along the way? So the primary role might be
helping to overcome informational or logistical

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.840
challenges, but oftentimes they're doing other
things in the process, right. And so the goal

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.129
is to say, look, that's not going to ever
be a line. But conceptually, it's helpful

00:03:59.129 --> 00:04:04.130
to understand companies that are primarily
engaged in this business of connecting and

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:08.090
Amazon and Walmart are among them. They certainly
have other activities that they engage in.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:13.239
Amazon, with something like Amazon Web Services,
is providing a completely different type of

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:20.029
value proposition. But much of what they do
is make it so American consumers can very

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:25.901
readily access and have enough information
to trust to buy goods that were made on the

00:04:25.901 --> 00:04:29.819
other side of the world. So there's efficiencies
there and there's a real connection that's

00:04:29.819 --> 00:04:33.240
provided. But there's drawbacks.
Speaker 1: One of the things that I think

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.020
is a concern for many in my audience, from
the things we've talked about, is the ways

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:45.210
in which sometimes very large corporations
will use middlemen and or contractors to avoid

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:50.620
accountability, in some cases, sometimes point
the finger at some other entity. The Amazon

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:56.210
delivery services is one where often the vans
that we all see driving around are not actually

00:04:56.210 --> 00:05:00.169
owned and operated by Amazon. Even if they
have Amazon painted. Sometimes it's local

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:04.619
LLC delivery companies that own a bunch of
vans or, you know, there's different things.

00:05:04.619 --> 00:05:09.729
Is this one of the concerns with this infrastructure
where you have all of these different parties?

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:15.189
Speaker 3: It is one of those very significant
concerns. So part of the challenge is, you

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:19.219
know, if you're just going directly to the
creator of a good not only there's a relationship

00:05:19.219 --> 00:05:23.590
there, but there's clear accountability and
any information you want about that good,

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:28.900
you can readily ascertain by asking the person
or asking for representations and warranties

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:34.319
if you want to be legalistic about the the
product, but also about the nature of how

00:05:34.319 --> 00:05:40.430
it was made and who was impacted. And part
of what the book shows is that much of the

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:45.169
complexity of today's supply chains and the
structure of these layers of intermediaries

00:05:45.169 --> 00:05:52.330
grew up at a time where we just assume investors
wanted to maximize the risk adjusted returns,

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:58.199
and consumers wanted nothing more than the
lowest price good holding quality constant

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:01.599
according to some objective measures. But
now we're in a world where people suddenly

00:06:01.599 --> 00:06:06.139
realize the government can't solve all of
our problems. We actually care more about

00:06:06.139 --> 00:06:13.860
the impact of the goods on the planet, on
the workers, and all through intentional use

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:20.499
of of middlemen to to gate, but also just
a byproduct of really complex chains that

00:06:20.499 --> 00:06:26.199
we're not necessarily designed to create opacity.
But as a practical matter, have created that

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:30.240
opacity. It is now virtually impossible at
times to understand. I mean, you can look

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.389
at your breakfast cereal and trying to figure
out where it all came from is oftentimes beyond

00:06:35.389 --> 00:06:39.449
the average consumers capability. And so as
we're bringing these additional values that

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:45.150
enable the accountability that people now
want is oftentimes very difficult, if not

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.599
impossible to glob on top of the current system.
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, in the kind of like

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:54.740
hipster lefty space that I occupy, there's
been a big move in the last five years to

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:59.979
go towards, you know, craft and independent
producers and products that are not as widely

00:06:59.979 --> 00:07:04.360
available. Sometimes that in and of itself
is what makes them more interesting. And and

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:09.520
I do think that there is value to that because,
you know, if you think about, like the coffee

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.840
roasting industry, instead of going to your
grocery store and grabbing the, you know,

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:19.649
a leg row that's available nationally or whatever,
you find some local roaster and it's more

00:07:19.649 --> 00:07:24.960
interesting and you know who's roasting it,
but it's almost always more expensive. And

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:32.270
so it it does seem that there is a very clear
advantage, at least in the minds of consumers

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:35.590
to that. But there is certainly a price associated
with it.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:39.580
Speaker 3: Yeah, as I want to say a couple
of things on that. So first, I love the coffee

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:43.749
roasting example. When I examples my box is
Reese Roasters, which was a local coffee roaster,

00:07:43.749 --> 00:07:48.180
you know, and I knew them when they were small
and they kept growing. But it is really nice.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:51.129
And one of the nice things about going direct
is even though it's more expensive, you not

00:07:51.129 --> 00:07:54.419
only learn a lot, but you know where the money's
going. So I actually feel a little better

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:57.680
about the money I'm spending because I know
the company. I know it's a local business.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:01.719
I know all of the people involved. I know
how they treat their employees. So so I actually

00:08:01.719 --> 00:08:07.050
feel better about the money I spent. And that
is partly shifting from a world where we had

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:12.399
long assumed scarcity to one where as a practical
matter, even though we still have a scarcity

00:08:12.399 --> 00:08:16.809
mindset because that's how we're taught to
think so often we really live in a world of

00:08:16.809 --> 00:08:21.610
abundance. So making more selective choices
is oftentimes more rewarding, which is part

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:25.479
of why you're seeing the trends you are. The
final point I would make is even though that's

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:29.759
very often true, that right now direct is
a little bit of a privilege. Good or seems

00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:35.941
like a privilege. Good? Yeah. It's more pervasive
than people realize outside of cities. So

00:08:35.941 --> 00:08:39.270
I get this question all the time from people
who live in relatively urban environments.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:44.270
I live in an urban environment, but right
now about our country. And part of what's

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:47.960
been interesting since I published the book
is I'm hearing from people in South Carolina

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:52.330
and Ohio in all of these different states
where they say this is exactly what's already

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:58.240
happening in my community. And actually, they're
they're keeping all of the value in the community.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.320
They're not necessarily paying as much as
a premium because it's not the craft artisan

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:07.720
that we think about in Brooklyn or or kind
of like a a neighborhood as much as somebody

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:12.610
who's really just like trying to create make
additional money by doing a product or service

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:17.800
on the side and maintains competitive by having
good prices. It's to be as cheap as the mass

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:22.690
produce good in Wal-Mart, partly because they're
you're exploiting kind of these incredibly

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:31.680
low wage and worker protections in other places.
But you really can. I think he hit a broader

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.800
set of the value spectrum that many of us
appreciate.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:41.700
Speaker 1: When it comes to supply chain and
some of the issues that we saw early in the

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:46.680
pandemic and then sort of again and then even
now to some degree, and we've seen baby formula

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:52.510
and different things. It's not completely
obvious to me as someone who does not share

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:57.630
your level of expertize in this issue, whether
the problem was made worse or was actually

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:03.940
resolved more quickly by the kind of status
quo of many middlemen in terms of supply chains

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:08.780
and the way that products kind of get to the
shelves and ultimately into people's homes.

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:12.320
What is the relationship or the connection
between those supply chain issues we've seen

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.360
over the last three years and the existence
of middlemen?

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:21.530
Speaker 3: So I would say in terms of the
the risk, what we've seen arise, it really

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:26.560
can be traced back to the proliferation of
middlemen. But even the middlemen, as you

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:31.150
point out, I think, accurately played a role
in helping us to address the challenges. Part

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:39.280
of what the direct documents is the way middlemen
proliferated slowly in number two become more

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:45.610
layered in ways that really created these
complex ecosystems. And generally speaking,

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:50.110
nobody took a step back to look at the entire
complex ecosystem. They were thinking, we

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:54.570
can eke a little more cost savings here, a
little more efficiency there. And so they

00:10:54.570 --> 00:11:00.860
kept making decisions that incrementally were
small but in aggregate transform the nature

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:07.180
of the system. And then, as we saw, once there
were shifts, whether it is making demand or

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:11.960
or limitations of supply, because countries
of origins, you know, were facing shutdowns.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:19.051
There were ripple effects that magnified that
original distortion because you had all of

00:11:19.051 --> 00:11:24.790
these suddenly individual players that had
depended on the system working well, having

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:29.490
to figure out how to signal, you know, what
they wanted, what they needed in ways where

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:33.350
they really looking out for themselves. And
so you suddenly ended up with people wanting,

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:36.970
demanding for more than they actually needed
because they're trying to hoard. And really,

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:40.930
the distortions kept getting bigger. And what's
interesting and for me is that this was actually

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:44.720
one of the original motivations for the book.
A lot of my original work was on financial

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:50.970
regulation and looking at the way securitization
chains that proliferated in the early aughts

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:55.770
contributed to the oh seven and oh eight crisis.
And we saw the exact same thing there where

00:11:55.770 --> 00:12:00.730
we saw these chains that grew. So they were
capital supply chains as opposed to good supply

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:05.430
chains. But they grew incrementally over time.
They appeared to make credit more available

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:08.672
so more people could buy homes. And it seemed
like a really good thing at the time because

00:12:08.672 --> 00:12:14.050
we could suddenly tap in to these new pools
of capital in Germany and Japan to fund American

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:20.620
homes. But we created an incredibly complex
ecosystem where once things broke down a little,

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:27.540
the dysfunction magnified and nobody had the
information they needed to help stabilize

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:30.260
the functioning.
Speaker 1: Yeah, it seems there's this interesting

